Chapter: CodexWasmHarness

 THE NATIVE FOURTH ARM. Since Update 62 this runs the driver's IR front door,
 `compile-frontend-ir` then `prepare-method-ir` (opening.codex), and hands
 what it prepared to `emit-wasm-chapter-stream`, where codex-zig-transpiler's
 CodexZigHarness hands the same thing to the zig emitter by way of the text.
 The property that makes it worth having is that this program runs the same
 code in the same order as the pipeline it stands in for, and the surest way
 to keep that is to CALL the pipeline rather than copy it.

 THE WIRE ROUND TRIP IS GONE, and this is the record of what it was doing.

 This driver used to emit the whole IR as text and parse it straight back
 before emitting, and said that was load-bearing: that the wire DERIVED what
 the AST does not carry, naming a computation of implicit type parameters in
 `IRTextEmitter`. That claim is not in the code -- both sides quote and parse
 the tparam list they are given. What the wire really derived was a positional
 FIELD SLOT, resolved against the receiver's record type and written into the
 field name as `parse-bag/14`, and the wasm plug read it rather than computing
 it. Its fallback when there was none searched every type-def by NAME and took
 the first match, which is wrong for the 59 field names in this subject that
 occur at more than one slot -- 73% of the emitted bytes.

 The plug now asks the receiver's type first and keeps the wire's number as a
 fallback (`wasm-slot-from-type`), which is the same number bare metal
 computes for itself in `X86_64Compound` and the same one the zig plug throws
 away. With that, the round trip derives nothing this arm needs.

 It cost 945 MB of 3,532 MB -- 27% -- and dropping it is byte-identical on
 the compiler's own 2.9 MB source and on 29 units of the safari port up to
 13.5 MB of WAT. `docs/the-ir-text-wire.md` is the measurement.

 THIS DRIVER IS NO LONGER THE SAME SHAPE AS THE GUEST ARM, and that is the
 cost paid. safari-codex's `harness/wasm_arm.py` compiles to IR on the seed
 and parses that text back before emitting; this one does not, so the two are
 no longer step-for-step comparable. The byte comparison above is what stands
 in for that, and it is a stronger check than the resemblance was.

 IT USED TO BE A COPY, of the driver's tail -- lift, prune to a copied roots
 list, and our own reading of declared exports -- and copies go stale without
 breaking loudly. By U62 it had drifted three ways: it never attached the
 equality helpers the driver has attached on the IR path since Update 56
 (COMPILER-44); it had no `method-materialize` (U62); and it read
 `wasm-exports` as a LIST of Text, a shape upstream's emitter has refused since
 Update 59 in favour of one Text literal, `"|name|name|"`, so for every valid
 declaration it rooted no export and the exported functions were pruned.
 Nothing here declares exports, so the fixed point could not see it.
 `prepare-method-ir` prunes with `ir-roots-with-declared`, which is upstream's
 reading of the same declaration, and all three are the driver's now.

Section: Halt

 The driver this stands in for does not emit when the bag has errors:
 opening.codex:1676-1678 prints the codegen error header and the notices,
 and then `if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED:
 errors in bag; no IR emitted"`. This harness skipped that gate until
 2026-08-25, and a cold read found what it costs: a file reading `this is
 not codex at all` produced 36,697 bytes of plausible wasm and exit 0, and so
 did a program calling a function that does not exist. For a tool that is
 handed to other people, the first thing they will feed it is a program with
 a mistake in it.

 The bags are the ones the driver merges, reached from what the frontend
 already binds: lex errors from `toks.errors` (opening.codex:449 wraps the
 same list), the parse bag from `doc.parse-bag` (:485), the resolve bag from
 `rr.bag`, and the type checker's from `cr.state.bag` -- which is what
 `check-bag0` at :620 merges. The PRINTERS are not reachable: they live in
 opening.codex, which cannot be bundled beside a harness defining `opening`,
 so this prints the count and the first error rather than the driver's full
 report.

 CODEGEN-HALTED is the marker the rest of the tree already refuses on by
 name (ast/f4_boot.py), which is why the text is that and not a new word.

  cwm-halted : List Diagnostic -> Text
  cwm-halted (es) =
   let n = list-length es
   in let e0 = list-at es 0
   in "CODEGEN-HALTED: " & show n & " error(s); no wasm emitted; first CDX" & show (e0.code) & " " & (e0.message) & "\n"

  cwm-sev-word : Integer -> Text
  cwm-sev-word (s) = if s == sev-warning then "warning" else "notice"

  cwm-notice-line : Diagnostic -> Text
  cwm-notice-line (d) = "CDX" & show (d.code) & " " & cwm-sev-word (d.severity) & ": " & (d.message)

  cwm-line-bytes : Text -> List Integer
  cwm-line-bytes (t) = list-push (text-to-utf8-bytes t) 10

  cwm-notices : List Diagnostic, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cwm-notices (ds) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length ds then acc
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if (d.severity) == sev-info then cwm-notices ds (i + 1) acc
   else cwm-notices ds (i + 1) (acc & cwm-line-bytes (cwm-notice-line d))

  cwm-info-count : List Diagnostic, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cwm-info-count (ds) (i) (n) =
   if i >= list-length ds then n
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if (d.severity) == sev-info then cwm-info-count ds (i + 1) (n + 1)
   else cwm-info-count ds (i + 1) n

  cwm-report : DiagnosticBag -> List Integer
  cwm-report (b) =
   let ds = bag-diagnostics b
   in let body = cwm-notices ds 0 []
   in let n = cwm-info-count ds 0 0
   in if n == 0 then body
   else body & cwm-line-bytes ("codexwasm: " & show n & " info diagnostics not shown")


Section: Driver

 The deck prologue is this harness's own and stays: the hosted reservation in
 front of the driver's phases, as CodexZigHarness carries.

 NOT the IR text. `prepare-method-ir` returns the pruned IRChapter and its
 type-defs, and nothing on that path renders the wire, so the round trip this
 driver dropped (above) stays dropped. `prepared.meta` is the wire's metadata
 and has no reader here.

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
    src <- read-file-uni "/dev/stdin"
    let mountain-base = init-phase-allocator
    in let deck-base = __heap-save
    in let deck-set = __deck-set deck-base
    in let deck-adv = __heap-advance 536870912
    in let fe = compile-frontend-ir src "Program" compile-flags-default
    in let prepared = prepare-method-ir fe compile-flags-default
    in if bag-has-errors (prepared.bag) then print-text (cwm-halted (bag-errors (prepared.bag)))
    else act
      write-binary (cwm-report (prepared.bag))
      emit-wasm-chapter-stream (prepared.chapter) (prepared.type-defs)
    end
  end
